<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!-- THE SECOND "Powered by Odoo", and the reason this file exists.

         Removing the login card's hardcoded odoo.com link (login_templates.xml)
         is enough on the databases we ship. It is NOT enough on a database with
         `website` installed, and that was measured rather than assumed: with
         website on, the login page came back carrying TWO attributions —

             odoo.com count: 2

         — because website replaces the login card with `website.layout`, whose
         footer calls `web.brand_promotion`, which calls
         `web.brand_promotion_message` (web/views/webclient_templates.xml:87).
         That template builds "Powered by %s" around an <img> of the Odoo logo
         badged with a utm_medium link.

         The same template is what the customer PORTAL and survey pages use, so
         overriding it here fixes every surface that emits the line, once,
         instead of chasing them one page at a time.

         THE PORTAL IS DELIBERATELY IN SCOPE. DO NOT NARROW THIS BACK TO THE
         LOGIN PAGE.

         It reads like over-reach — the brief that prompted this change was
         about the login page — so the reasoning is recorded here rather than
         left to be re-derived by whoever next reads a diff and thinks the
         portal was collateral:

         The login page is seen by the CLIENT'S OWN STAFF, who know perfectly
         well what software they bought. The customer portal is seen by the
         CLIENT'S CUSTOMERS: the quotation they accept, the invoice they pay,
         the delivery they track. That is the one screen in the whole product a
         third party looks at, and it is the screen where an "Powered by Odoo"
         badge tells a client's customer whose software this really is.

         So the portal is MORE brand-sensitive than the login page, not less.
         A change that de-brands the login page and leaves the portal alone has
         fixed the private screen and left the public one.

         If this ever does need scoping, scope it by adding a login-only
         override — do not delete this one.

         `_message` is dropped deliberately. Callers pass Odoo marketing copy
         through it (website_sale passes "the #1 Open Source eCommerce"), and
         there is no version of that sentence which belongs on a client's ERP.

         This is template inheritance on an LGPL-3 module, not a change to it.
         See README.md, "Odoo attribution", for the licensing position. -->

    <template id="brand_promotion_message" inherit_id="web.brand_promotion_message"
              name="SapianERP brand promotion">
        <!-- The Odoo logo <a> lives inside a t-set body, so it never reaches
             the page once the t-out below is replaced. Emptied anyway: a
             variable that still builds a competitor's link is a trap for
             whoever inherits this next. -->
        <xpath expr="//t[@t-set='odoo_logo']" position="replace">
            <t t-set="odoo_logo"/>
        </xpath>
        <xpath expr="//t[@t-set='final_message']" position="replace">
            <t t-set="final_message"/>
        </xpath>
        <!-- EXACTLY ONE MARK ON THE LOGIN PAGE, and this is the half that
             stands down there.

             On a database with `website`, /web/login is rendered inside
             `website.layout`, whose footer calls `web.brand_promotion` ->
             `web.brand_promotion_message` — this template. The login form ALSO
             carries its own attribution, anchored on the form itself in
             login_templates.xml, because on a database WITHOUT `website` that
             is the only thing that renders. So with website installed the page
             came back with TWO. Measured on the 36-app database:

                 /web/login           sapian marks: 2
                 /web/signup          sapian marks: 1
                 /web/reset_password  sapian marks: 1

             The login form's own mark wins, for the reason the scoping is
             narrow: it is the one that renders on EVERY database, website or
             not, so making it conditional would trade a duplicate for an
             absence. This one is suppressed on /web/login alone.

             /web/signup and /web/reset_password extend `web.login_layout` and
             not `web.login`, so the form xpath never reaches them and THIS
             template is their only attribution — which is why the path test is
             an exact match on /web/login rather than a prefix on /web/log.
             Both are asserted in check_login_page.py.

             The portal keeps its mark, unconditionally. That was a deliberate
             decision recorded above and it is not narrowed here: the only page
             this touches is the login page, which has its own. -->
        <xpath expr="//t[@t-out]" position="replace">
            <t t-set="sapian_login_owns_mark"
               t-value="bool(request) and request.httprequest.path.rstrip('/') == '/web/login'"/>
            <span t-if="not sapian_login_owns_mark" class="o_sapian_powered">
                <a href="https://sapiantech.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                    <t t-call="sapian_theme.mark"/>
                    <span>Powered by SapianERP</span>
                </a>
            </span>
        </xpath>
    </template>

</odoo>
